1
00:00:07,799 --> 00:00:14,810
Wen Shulan was ninety-nine years old in 2077. She had
retired from the Ministry of State

2
00:00:14,830 --> 00:00:21,680
Security in 2061, had taken Aeterna in 2065 at the
insistence of her daughter, and had

3
00:00:21,700 --> 00:00:28,791
spent sixteen years in a house outside Hangzhou
writing a history that she did not intend

4
00:00:28,811 --> 00:00:29,669
to publish.

5
00:00:30,070 --> 00:00:35,709
She had not been consulted about the Coalition. Her
country had not joined it; that had

6
00:00:35,729 --> 00:00:41,629
been her doing, from retirement, in four conversations
over two years, and it is the reason

7
00:00:41,649 --> 00:00:45,728
China was one of the two abstentions on
the fifteenth of March.

8
00:00:46,128 --> 00:00:52,424
In September Albert Wesker came to see her, and it
was the fifth time they had met in

9
00:00:52,444 --> 00:00:53,613
forty-one years.

10
00:00:54,134 --> 00:00:56,163
You look precisely the same.

11
00:00:56,564 --> 00:00:59,653
You look better than in 2036.

12
00:01:00,054 --> 00:01:06,101
I am better than in 2036. That is your doing and I
have never thanked you for it and I

13
00:01:06,121 --> 00:01:07,724
do not intend to start.

14
00:01:08,123 --> 00:01:14,159
She poured tea. The narrator notes that neither of
them had said anything about the Eleven

15
00:01:14,179 --> 00:01:19,070
Days for four minutes, which was, for both of
them, a form of politeness.

16
00:01:19,590 --> 00:01:21,430
They should not have done it.

17
00:01:21,831 --> 00:01:23,181
They were right to do it.

18
00:01:23,581 --> 00:01:29,518
Both of those are true, which is the whole
difficulty. I told four ministers in 2073 that

19
00:01:29,538 --> 00:01:35,341
it would fail and that the failure would make him
unassailable, and I was told I was an

20
00:01:35,361 --> 00:01:39,491
old woman who had been compromised by a
personal relationship.

21
00:01:40,100 --> 00:01:45,232
I was an old woman who had watched you for forty-one
years and had noticed that you have

22
00:01:45,252 --> 00:01:50,441
never once been beaten by anyone who came at you
directly, and that on the four occasions

23
00:01:50,461 --> 00:01:54,071
you were beaten it was by someone who
asked you for something.

24
00:01:54,471 --> 00:01:55,921
She put the pot down.

25
00:01:56,441 --> 00:01:59,031
So I am going to ask you for something.

26
00:01:59,430 --> 00:02:05,748
What Wen Shulan proposed on the eleventh of
September 2077 became, after two years and

27
00:02:05,768 --> 00:02:12,012
four months of negotiation, the Hangzhou Accord,
and the narrator will give it to you

28
00:02:12,032 --> 00:02:14,371
in the four clauses that matter.

29
00:02:14,770 --> 00:02:20,641
One. The Umbrella Directorate is recognised, by
every signatory, as a legal person in

30
00:02:20,661 --> 00:02:26,531
international law with the capacity to hold
territory, conclude treaties and be sued.

31
00:02:26,551 --> 00:02:32,630
Not a state. A new category, and the argument about
what to call it lasted eleven months

32
00:02:32,650 --> 00:02:35,540
and was settled with the word *Authority*.

33
00:02:35,941 --> 00:02:42,268
Two. Umbrella's core functions — medicine, energy,
water, the strategic reserves — are

34
00:02:42,288 --> 00:02:48,984
placed under a standing obligation of supply. It may
not withhold them from any population,

35
00:02:49,004 --> 00:02:55,479
for any reason, ever. The obligation is enforceable
in a tribunal that Umbrella does not

36
00:02:55,499 --> 00:02:57,251
appoint and cannot veto.

37
00:02:57,651 --> 00:03:04,027
Three. Every Umbrella territory retains the right to
leave, by referendum, on two years'

38
00:03:04,047 --> 00:03:09,260
notice, and the signatories guarantee the
independence of any that does.

39
00:03:09,661 --> 00:03:16,172
Four — and this is Wen's clause, the one she wrote
herself in a garden in Hangzhou, and

40
00:03:16,192 --> 00:03:20,450
the one that Albert Wesker argued
about for nine months —

41
00:03:20,969 --> 00:03:26,886
The Authority shall maintain no capacity to compel
the obedience of any human being by

42
00:03:26,906 --> 00:03:33,237
means that the person cannot perceive, refuse or
report. Existing personnel in that category

43
00:03:33,257 --> 00:03:37,379
shall be enumerated, registered, and
progressively released.

44
00:03:38,330 --> 00:03:42,300
(very cold) You are asking me to
give up the Absolute.

45
00:03:42,699 --> 00:03:48,464
I am asking you to stop *making* them, and to let the
four hundred thousand you have made

46
00:03:48,484 --> 00:03:50,609
go, over whatever period is safe.

47
00:03:51,009 --> 00:03:57,788
Half of them will not survive it. You have read the
Coalition's own analysis; you know

48
00:03:57,808 --> 00:04:04,903
what happened at Teraina in eleven days. These are not
slaves I can free with a signature.

49
00:04:04,923 --> 00:04:11,938
They are people who have never once in their lives
held their own reins, and some of them

50
00:04:11,958 --> 00:04:13,519
are forty years old.

51
00:04:13,919 --> 00:04:19,338
I know. That is why the clause says *progressively*,
and why there is no deadline in it,

52
00:04:19,358 --> 00:04:22,490
and why I have given you the right
to set the pace.

53
00:04:23,209 --> 00:04:28,178
(very slow) Albert. I have watched you for forty-one
years, and in all that time there

54
00:04:28,198 --> 00:04:33,283
is exactly one thing you have built that I would call
evil, and it is not the virus, and

55
00:04:33,303 --> 00:04:37,053
it is not the soldiers, and it is not the
monster in the stadium.

56
00:04:37,334 --> 00:04:42,766
It is that you solved the oldest problem of power —
that people can say no to you — and

57
00:04:42,786 --> 00:04:47,904
you solved it so completely that you have
forgotten it was a problem worth having.

58
00:04:48,305 --> 00:04:54,688
The narrator will record that Albert Wesker did not
answer for a long time, and that what

59
00:04:54,708 --> 00:04:57,134
he finally said was not agreement.

60
00:04:57,654 --> 00:05:03,822
(very soft) If I sign that clause, I will spend
forty years dismantling the thing that

61
00:05:03,842 --> 00:05:10,081
made all of it possible, and there is a real chance
that it kills a great many of them,

62
00:05:10,101 --> 00:05:16,413
and the only reason I would do it is that a
ninety-nine-year-old woman in a garden asked

63
00:05:16,433 --> 00:05:16,844
me to.

64
00:05:17,245 --> 00:05:18,206
Yes.

65
00:05:18,605 --> 00:05:20,695
That is a very poor reason.

66
00:05:21,095 --> 00:05:26,991
It is the only kind of reason anybody has ever had.
You have been waiting since 2026 for

67
00:05:27,011 --> 00:05:32,907
somebody to be able to make you do something. Nobody
can. So it will have to be somebody

68
00:05:32,927 --> 00:05:33,580
you *let*.

69
00:05:34,639 --> 00:05:41,292
He signed it in Hangzhou on the fourth of January
2080, with forty-one signatory states,

70
00:05:41,312 --> 00:05:43,870
in a room with four cameras in it.

71
00:05:44,269 --> 00:05:50,375
The Registry's letter went to four hundred and eleven
thousand people over thirty years,

72
00:05:50,395 --> 00:05:56,500
in the order in which their cohorts came up, and the
narrator has read the fourth draft,

73
00:05:56,520 --> 00:06:02,695
which is the one that was used, and which Albert
Wesker wrote himself in 2081 and rewrote

74
00:06:02,715 --> 00:06:03,531
three times.

75
00:06:03,930 --> 00:06:09,974
It is nine paragraphs. It does not apologise, which
was the most argued-about decision

76
00:06:09,994 --> 00:06:12,230
in the Directorate for a decade.

77
00:06:12,630 --> 00:06:18,345
It says: that the recipient was placed into the world
on a specific date, which is given;

78
00:06:18,365 --> 00:06:23,822
that their memories before that date were provided
rather than lived; that everything

79
00:06:23,842 --> 00:06:29,364
since is entirely their own; that the people in
their life are real and their feelings

80
00:06:29,384 --> 00:06:35,485
for them are real; that they are, in law and in every
way the Directorate is able to determine,

81
00:06:35,505 --> 00:06:41,156
a person; that they have never been able to refuse an
instruction from Umbrella and that

82
00:06:41,176 --> 00:06:46,891
this will end on a date, which is given; that they
are entitled to see every record about

83
00:06:46,911 --> 00:06:52,432
themselves, and to compensation, and to leave with a
pension at any time; and that the

84
00:06:52,452 --> 00:06:58,360
man responsible is named, and is alive, and will answer
any letter they send him personally.

85
00:06:58,761 --> 00:07:01,081
About sixty-one thousand of them wrote.

86
00:07:01,480 --> 00:07:07,676
He answered all sixty-one thousand, over
thirty-nine years, in longhand, at a rate of

87
00:07:07,696 --> 00:07:13,965
about nine a week, and the archive of those replies
is four hundred volumes and is the

88
00:07:13,985 --> 00:07:16,378
other reason this account exists.

89
00:07:16,778 --> 00:07:22,932
Ray Dorsey — the plant supervisor from Chapter Four,
who had telephoned his wife on his

90
00:07:22,952 --> 00:07:29,388
first Monday and told her he had been doing this for
twenty-seven years — was in the second

91
00:07:29,408 --> 00:07:32,369
cohort, at the age of fifty-five, in 2082.

92
00:07:32,769 --> 00:07:36,419
He wrote four lines. The reply is
in volume nine.

93
00:07:36,939 --> 00:07:41,769
*I don't want compensation and I don't want to leave.
I want to know one thing. The holiday

94
00:07:41,789 --> 00:07:43,369
in Ocean City. Did it happen?*

95
00:07:43,769 --> 00:07:51,712
*Mr. Dorsey. No. It did not happen, and I am sorry to
be the one to confirm it. You were

96
00:07:51,732 --> 00:07:59,403
given it complete, including the rain on the
Thursday, on the eighth of January 2027,

97
00:07:59,423 --> 00:08:07,366
because a system I do not fully understand decided
that a man of your age would have had

98
00:08:07,386 --> 00:08:07,819
one.*

99
00:08:08,098 --> 00:08:14,471
*I have thought about your question for some days. I
think the honest answer is that the

100
00:08:14,491 --> 00:08:20,717
holiday is the only thing in your life that is not
yours, and that everything you have

101
00:08:20,737 --> 00:08:26,528
done with your wife since that Monday is, and
that there is rather a lot of it.*

102
00:08:26,809 --> 00:08:34,807
(very slow) *You are entitled to be angry with me
and I will read anything further you

103
00:08:34,827 --> 00:08:36,579
send. — A. Wesker.*

104
00:08:36,979 --> 00:08:42,866
Ray Dorsey took his wife to Ocean City in the
spring of 2083. He wrote once more, two

105
00:08:42,886 --> 00:08:48,912
lines, and the reply in volume nine is one line, and
the narrator will not print either

106
00:08:48,932 --> 00:08:54,819
of them, because there are four hundred volumes of
this and some of it belongs to the

107
00:08:54,839 --> 00:08:56,209
people who wrote it.

108
00:08:56,609 --> 00:09:01,485
The enumeration took four years. Four hundred
and eleven thousand names.

109
00:09:01,884 --> 00:09:07,803
The release programme took the rest of the century
and is not finished at the end of this

110
00:09:07,823 --> 00:09:13,742
account. The narrator will give you its shape,
because it is the only thing Albert Wesker

111
00:09:13,762 --> 00:09:19,481
ever did that he did badly, and he did it badly for
twenty-seven years before it began

112
00:09:19,501 --> 00:09:20,015
to work.

113
00:09:20,414 --> 00:09:26,610
The first cohort was nine hundred volunteers at
Raccoon City in 2081: Absolute personnel

114
00:09:26,630 --> 00:09:32,614
in non-critical roles, released in the only way
anyone could think of, which was that

115
00:09:32,634 --> 00:09:38,618
Wesker issued a final instruction saying *from this
date you will take no instruction

116
00:09:38,638 --> 00:09:44,904
from me or from anyone in my name*, and then the
Directorate waited to see what happened.

117
00:09:45,304 --> 00:09:51,086
Fourteen of the nine hundred could not tolerate it.
The narrator will not describe what

118
00:09:51,106 --> 00:09:57,021
that means; the medical literature calls it terminal
executive vacancy and there are four

119
00:09:57,041 --> 00:09:59,754
papers about it and all of them are grim.

120
00:10:00,154 --> 00:10:06,312
The other eight hundred and eighty-six were, within
about nine months, indistinguishable

121
00:10:06,332 --> 00:10:12,489
from any other citizen of Raccoon City, and one of
them opened a bakery, and one of them

122
00:10:12,509 --> 00:10:17,965
ran for the city council in 2086 and lost by
four hundred votes and ran again.

123
00:10:18,365 --> 00:10:24,539
Evelyn Marsh's group rebuilt the programme four
times. The version that worked — from

124
00:10:24,559 --> 00:10:30,879
2087 — was not medical at all. It was an
apprenticeship: two years in which an Absolute

125
00:10:30,899 --> 00:10:37,729
employee was required, daily, to make one small decision
that nobody had authorised, beginning

126
00:10:37,749 --> 00:10:39,769
with what to have for lunch.

127
00:10:40,169 --> 00:10:42,639
The failure rate fell to under
one per cent.

128
00:10:43,039 --> 00:10:49,374
Gus was in the ninth cohort, in 2088, at his own
request, at the age of sixty-two years

129
00:10:49,394 --> 00:10:55,509
and one hundred and thirty-one days, all of which
he had spent in the same building.

130
00:10:55,909 --> 00:11:02,291
Wesker came to Wilmington for it. He had come for
all nine cohorts; he came for every

131
00:11:02,311 --> 00:11:09,220
cohort until the end of the account, and it is the only
appointment he never once delegated.

132
00:11:09,740 --> 00:11:15,313
Director. They tell me I'm to ask you a question,
and I've had eleven days to think of

133
00:11:15,333 --> 00:11:17,070
one, and I haven't got one.

134
00:11:17,470 --> 00:11:20,740
Then ask me the one you had on
the first morning.

135
00:11:21,470 --> 00:11:28,634
That lift button. In 2026. I cleaned the contacts on
a Tuesday and I've never once known

136
00:11:28,654 --> 00:11:29,777
whether I did.

137
00:11:30,177 --> 00:11:36,413
(very soft) You did not. The sublevel did not exist
until three o'clock on the Wednesday

138
00:11:36,433 --> 00:11:42,527
morning, and you were given the memory of cleaning
it along with the keys, and I stood

139
00:11:42,547 --> 00:11:48,569
in the lobby on the Wednesday and watched you tell
me about it and I knew, and I said

140
00:11:48,589 --> 00:11:53,688
nothing, and I have thought about that
conversation for sixty-two years.

141
00:11:54,638 --> 00:11:55,339
Right.

142
00:11:55,618 --> 00:11:58,854
Well. It's a good building,
whatever's under it.

143
00:11:59,254 --> 00:12:05,406
The narrator notes that Gus took the apprenticeship,
that he passed it in fourteen months,

144
00:12:05,426 --> 00:12:11,440
that he stayed on at the campus as head of
maintenance at a salary he negotiated himself

145
00:12:11,460 --> 00:12:17,885
and won four per cent on, and that when he died in 2103
— actually died, of being ninety-seven

146
00:12:17,905 --> 00:12:23,713
and having declined Aeterna twice — the Directorate
flew its flag at half mast, which

147
00:12:23,733 --> 00:12:26,524
it had done for exactly one other person.

148
00:12:27,674 --> 00:12:35,677
The Hangzhou Accord. Forty-one signatory states.
Recognition: Authority. Obligation of

149
00:12:35,697 --> 00:12:43,607
supply: binding, enforceable, not vetoable. Four
hundred and eleven thousand Absolute

150
00:12:43,627 --> 00:12:50,510
personnel enumerated; release programme begun.
Points: minus nine billion.

151
00:12:51,650 --> 00:12:54,620
(very cold) You have taken Points off me.

152
00:12:55,020 --> 00:12:55,831
Yes.

153
00:12:56,230 --> 00:13:00,740
In fifty-four years you have never once
taken Points off me.

154
00:13:01,140 --> 00:13:08,862
(flat) You have surrendered compelled obedience,
which is the largest single asset I have

155
00:13:08,882 --> 00:13:16,255
ever recorded a host holding, and the ledger is
honest. I told you that in 2028 about

156
00:13:16,275 --> 00:13:19,125
a city and it is true about this.

157
00:13:19,966 --> 00:13:21,803
Was it the wrong decision?

158
00:13:22,863 --> 00:13:25,949
(flat) I do not evaluate. I price.

159
00:13:26,229 --> 00:13:33,067
Nine billion is the price of what you gave away. I
would draw your attention to the fact

160
00:13:33,087 --> 00:13:39,925
that it is the only transaction in fifty-four years
in which you have paid for something

161
00:13:39,945 --> 00:13:42,107
that does not belong to you.

162
00:13:42,508 --> 00:13:48,015
The narrator notes that Albert Wesker stood at that
window for some time, in a building

163
00:13:48,035 --> 00:13:53,480
that had appeared out of nothing on the fourteenth
of September 2026, and that what he

164
00:13:53,500 --> 00:13:59,071
said at the end of it was addressed to nobody in the
room, because the only other person

165
00:13:59,091 --> 00:14:04,472
who would have understood it had been silent inside
his own skull for eighteen years.

166
00:14:04,993 --> 00:14:09,143
(very soft) She asked me for it, and
I let her have it.

167
00:14:09,423 --> 00:14:13,853
That is twice now. It had better
not become a habit.
